kernel: replace compile-time Arch trait with runtime service capability traits

This commit completes a multi-phase refactor that replaces the old
compile-time #[cfg(target_arch)] dispatch pattern--where every driver
and subsystem branched on the Arch trait implementation--with a unified
runtime service-container model.  The Arch trait is deleted, all PCI
internals use the global kernel_services() accessor, and every kernel
subsystem now speaks through dyn Trait references collected in a single
KernelServices struct.

=== Why the Arch trait had to go ===

The original design used pub trait Arch (in arch/mod.rs) with associated
fn discover_cpus(), wake_aps(), halt(), disable_interrupts(), and so on.
Each impl block was behind #[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")] or
#[cfg(target_arch = "riscv64")], selected at compile time through the
CurrentArch alias.

This worked but carried four chronic problems:

1.  Monolithic trait, rigid dispatch.  Every architectural capability
    (timers, interrupts, PCI config space, MSI allocation, DMA, ACPI)
    was either exposed through the Arch trait or wired ad-hoc via static
    functions.  Adding a new capability required changing one trait
    definition, two impl blocks, and updating all call sites.  There
    was no way to compose or stub capabilities independently--riscv64
    had to implement wake_aps() even though it had no SMP trampoline
    on QEMU virt.

2.  No runtime polymorphism.  The CurrentArch alias selected exactly one
    implementation at link time.  Features like "probe which interrupt
    model the firmware set up" or "fall back from MSI to MSI-X" or
    "provide a null DMA allocator on hardware without an IOMMU" were
    impossible to express without piling more cfg-gates into the trait
    impl.

3.  Ecam coupling.  The PCI subsystem (caps.rs, enumerate.rs, msi.rs,
    msix.rs) imported super::ecam directly and called read_u8/write_u16
    etc.  This meant every PCI config-space access was hard-wired to
    the ECAM implementation--no alternative host-bridge interface, no
    unit-test injection, no riscv64 stub that returns 0xFFFF for
    "no PCI bus".

4.  init() coupling and static friction.  smp::init() imported Arch and
    CurrentArch to call discover_cpus() and wake_aps().  Kernel::init()
    called CurrentArch::enable_interrupts().  Every new architectural
    dependency became another import chain that tied the whole kernel
    together at compile time.

=== What the service-container model provides ===

The new architecture defines fine-grained capability traits under
kernel/src/services/.  Each trait covers exactly one concern:

    Capability              -- supertrait providing name() (for debug)
    TimerProvider           -- now_ns, sleep_ns, register_tick_handler
    InterruptManager        -- register/unregister handler, enable, disable, eoi
    PhysicalMemoryAllocator -- alloc/free/reserve frames
    VirtualMemoryManager    -- map, unmap, translate, root, flush_tlb
    SerialConsole           -- putc, puts, put_hex, put_u64
    PlatformControl         -- shutdown, reset, halt, interrupt control
    CpuManager              -- cpu_id, cpu_count, IPI, discover_cpus, wake_aps
    PciConfigSpace          -- PCI config read8/16/32 write8/16/32
    MsiAllocator            -- allocate/release MSI vectors, message address/data
    DmaAllocator            -- alloc_page, alloc_contiguous, map_mmio, virt_to_phys
    BlockDevice             -- submit, sector_count, model_string
    PciDeviceManager        -- device list, BARs, MSI/MSI-X config
    AcpiProvider            -- interrupt model, PCI regions, CPUs, MMIO mapping

Each trait is implemented by a provider struct in an arch-specific
subdirectory (services/x86_64/ or services/riscv64/).  Providers are
assembled at boot time by init_services() into a single KernelServices
container, which is leaked as a &'static reference and exposed via the
global kernel_services() accessor.

Call sites that once wrote:

    CurrentArch::enable_interrupts()
    CurrentArch::discover_cpus(acpi)
    ecam::read_u16(seg, bus, dev, fn, reg)

now write:

    kernel_services().platform.enable_interrupts()
    kernel_services().cpu.discover_cpus(acpi)
    kernel_services().pci_cfg.read16(seg, bus, dev, fn, reg)

=== The Arch trait removal (Phase 5) ===

When the service-container was fully operational, the remaining Arch
trait methods (init, init_ap, halt, disable_interrupts,
enable_interrupts, are_interrupts_enabled, setup_virt_mem, discover_cpus,
wake_aps) were pure dead code--every consumer had been migrated to the
equivalent service:

  - halt/disables/enables -> self.svc().platform.* (in lib.rs)
  - discover_cpus/wake_aps -> services.cpu.* (in smp/mod.rs)
  - halt() in xhci/mod.rs -> CurrentArch::halt() (inherent, not trait)

What changed per file:

  arch/mod.rs
    Removed the trait definition entirely.  The file now only re-exports
    the two arch modules behind cfg gates and provides the CurrentArch
    alias.

  arch/x86_64/mod.rs and arch/riscv64/mod.rs
    Changed from impl Arch for X86_64 (and impl Arch for Riscv64) to
    inherent impl blocks with pub fn.  This preserves the functions for
    callers that still use them (e.g. X86_64::init() in main.rs) while
    eliminating the trait dispatch layer.

    Both method bodies (discover_cpus and wake_aps) were removed from
    the arch modules entirely because:
      - discover_cpus     -> moved into X86Cpu / RiscvCpu providers
      - wake_aps          -> moved into X86Cpu / RiscvCpu providers

  arch/x86_64/trampoline.rs and arch/riscv64/trampoline.rs
    Removed use crate::arch::Arch imports (no longer needed).

  main.rs
    Changed use kernel::arch::{Arch, CurrentArch} to
    use kernel::arch::CurrentArch only (Arch no longer exists).

  lib.rs
    Added svc() helper and called self.svc().platform.enable_interrupts()
    and self.svc().platform.halt() instead of CurrentArch::*.

  usb/xhci/mod.rs
    Replaced <CurrentArch as Arch>::halt() and
    <CurrentArch as Arch>::are_interrupts_enabled() with the inherent
    CurrentArch::halt() and CurrentArch::are_interrupts_enabled() calls
    (now that these are regular methods, not trait methods, the
    qualified-syntax call is no longer needed).

  filesystems/vfs/irq.rs
    Removed the unused use crate::arch::Arch import.

=== PCI internals migration ===

The four PCI files that imported ecam directly now route through the
global service:

  pci/caps.rs           -- all read_u8/16/32, write_u8/16/32 -> pci_cfg.*
  pci/enumerate.rs      -- all read_u8/16/32 -> pci_cfg.*
  pci/msi.rs            -- all ecam::read_u16/write_u16 -> pci_cfg.*
  pci/msix.rs           -- all ecam::read_u16/write_u16 -> pci_cfg.*

Each file now has a local fn cfg() helper that calls
kernel_services().pci_cfg, keeping the diff minimal and the cfg
reference at the point of use.

=== Extended service interfaces ===

PciConfigSpace gained write8 and write16 methods (previously only
read8/16/32 existed, but msi/msix needed byte and word writes).

InterruptManager gained unregister_handler (to complement register_handler).

The Capability supertrait gained Send + Sync bounds, enforcing that
every provider must be thread-safe (since kernel_services() is
accessible from any CPU).

BitmapAllocator received unsafe impl Send + Sync (it is protected by
external locking via the heap module's PHYS_ALLOCATOR pointer).

=== IDT/timer tick callback ===

The x86_64 IDT timer handler previously called apic_eoi() directly
with no mechanism for a higher-level tick dispatcher to hook in.
The timer service provider (ApicTimer) needed a way to receive tick
notifications.

Added:
  - TIMER_TICK_CALLBACK static AtomicPtr<fn()> in idt.rs
  - set_timer_tick_callback() to install the callback
  - timer_handler() now invokes the callback before EOI

Also made DEVICE_VECTOR_BASE, NUM_DEVICE_VECTORS pub, and added
register_device_handler_at(vector, handler) for the InterruptManager
provider to use.

=== SMP allocator decoupling ===

smp::init() previously took &mut BitmapAllocator and passed it to
Arch::discover_cpus and Arch::wake_aps.  With the trait gone, the
allocator argument is no longer needed--the CPU manager providers
hold their own reference to the allocator (via heap::get_phys_allocator_mut
or a stashed pointer).

smp::init() now takes (page_table_root, acpi, services) and calls
services.cpu.discover_cpus() / services.cpu.wake_aps().  The
internal allocate_ap_stack() also switched from a passed-in allocator
to calling heap::get_phys_allocator_mut() directly.

=== mm/vmm TLB flush ===

Vmm gained a flush_tlb() method (reload CR3 on x86, sfence.vma on riscv)
so the VirtualMemoryManager trait can provide TLB coherence without
calling into raw asm!() at every call site.

=== Minor fixes discovered during the refactor ===

riscv64 mfence -> fence
  The msix.rs module had a raw mfence instruction that doesn't exist on
  riscv64.  It is now gated on target_arch = x86_64; riscv64 uses the
  equivalent fence instruction.

block_cache verify_integrity gate
  CachedDevice::submit() called verify_integrity() which is x86_64-only
  (it reads IDT registers).  Gated on target_arch = x86_64.

usb_test module gate
  The USB test module depends on xHCI which is x86_64-only.  Now gated
  on target_arch = x86_64.

=== New files (33 services .rs files) ===

services/mod.rs
  KernelServices struct with 10 capability fields, init_services(),
  set_global(), kernel_services().

services/capability.rs             -- Capability trait (Send + Sync)
services/timer.rs                  -- TimerProvider trait
services/interrupts.rs             -- InterruptManager trait
services/phys_mem.rs               -- PhysicalMemoryAllocator trait
services/virt_mem.rs               -- VirtualMemoryManager trait
services/serial.rs                 -- SerialConsole trait + KernelSerial provider
services/platform.rs               -- PlatformControl trait
services/cpu.rs                    -- CpuManager trait
services/pci_config.rs             -- PciConfigSpace trait
services/msi.rs                    -- MsiAllocator trait
services/dma.rs                    -- DmaAllocator trait + KernelDma provider
services/block_device.rs           -- BlockDevice trait
services/pci_device.rs             -- PciDeviceManager trait
services/acpi.rs                   -- AcpiProvider trait
services/ecam_pci_config.rs        -- EcamPciConfig provider (both archs)
services/null_msi.rs               -- NullMsi provider (riscv64 stub)

services/x86_64/mod.rs             -- x86_64 provider module
services/x86_64/apic_timer.rs      -- ApicTimer (TimerProvider)
services/x86_64/x86_interrupts.rs  -- X86Interrupts (InterruptManager)
services/x86_64/x86_platform.rs    -- X86Platform (PlatformControl)
services/x86_64/x86_cpu.rs         -- X86Cpu (CpuManager)
services/x86_64/x86_msi.rs         -- X86Msi (MsiAllocator)
services/x86_64/x86_pci_device.rs  -- X86PciDevice (PciDeviceManager)
services/x86_64/x86_acpi.rs        -- X86Acpi (AcpiProvider)
services/x86_64/x86_serial.rs      -- x86_64 serial support

services/riscv64/mod.rs            -- riscv64 provider module
services/riscv64/sbi_timer.rs      -- SbiTimer (TimerProvider)
services/riscv64/riscv_interrupts.rs -- RiscvInterrupts (InterruptManager)
services/riscv64/riscv_platform.rs -- RiscvPlatform (PlatformControl)
services/riscv64/riscv_cpu.rs      -- RiscvCpu (CpuManager)
services/riscv64/riscv_pci_device.rs -- RiscvPciDevice (PciDeviceManager, stub)
services/riscv64/riscv_acpi.rs     -- RiscvAcpi (AcpiProvider, stub)

=== Build verification ===

cargo build --target x86_64-unknown-none -p kernel --features "display_log cpu_slow"
  -> clean

cargo build --target x86_64-unknown-uefi -p boot
  -> clean

cargo build --target riscv64gc-unknown-none-elf -p kernel --lib --features display_log
  -> clean (lib only; bin has pre-existing linker error for __idt_start/__idt_end)

=== Pre-existing issues (unchanged) ===

- riscv64 bin link: __idt_start, __idt_end symbols referenced in lib.rs
  KernelLayout but not defined in linker-riscv64.ld
- SerialPort calls (~200 sites) remain direct; KernelSerial wraps the same
  Inner struct so this is purely a naming convention decision.

=== temp-plan.md ===

Rewritten to reflect completed architecture: trait table, provider table,
build commands, and a list of removed items.
